1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,745
Hand someone five twenty-dollar bills and

2
00:00:02,745 --> 00:00:04,285
the cash vanishes fast.

3
00:00:04,285 --> 00:00:06,895
Hand them one crisp hundred-dollar bill

4
00:00:06,895 --> 00:00:08,770
and their wallet stays shut.

5
00:00:08,770 --> 00:00:10,567
In a 2009 Journal of Consumer

6
00:00:10,567 --> 00:00:11,497
Research study,

7
00:00:11,497 --> 00:00:14,162
Priya Raghubir and Joydeep Srivastava named

8
00:00:14,162 --> 00:00:15,960
this the Denomination Effect:

9
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:18,191
twenty-six percent of people spent a

10
00:00:18,191 --> 00:00:19,369
single dollar bill,

11
00:00:19,369 --> 00:00:22,220
while sixty-three percent spent four quarters.

12
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:24,949
Tap-to-pay cards and one-click checkouts turn

13
00:00:24,949 --> 00:00:27,435
your entire checking balance into digital

14
00:00:27,435 --> 00:00:28,224
loose change,

15
00:00:28,224 --> 00:00:30,832
erasing the psychological sting of breaking

16
00:00:30,832 --> 00:00:31,620
a large bill.

17
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:33,554
Rebuild that boundary on purpose:

18
00:00:33,554 --> 00:00:35,956
sweep surplus cash into hundred-dollar or

19
00:00:35,956 --> 00:00:38,652
thousand-dollar blocks inside a separate vault

20
00:00:38,652 --> 00:00:40,703
account that requires a slow manual

21
00:00:40,703 --> 00:00:41,230
transfer.

22
00:00:41,230 --> 00:00:43,614
Once breaking a whole block feels

23
00:00:43,614 --> 00:00:44,626
painful again,

24
00:00:44,626 --> 00:00:46,865
impulse leaks dry up before you

25
00:00:46,865 --> 00:00:48,310
ever reach checkout.
